  "preamble": "The palato-alveolar fricative [ʒ] shifts to the retroflex (apical or laminal, non-palatalized) fricative [ʐ] in Russian, Belarusian and Polish. Proto-Slavic *ž, a palato-alveolar sound produced by the first palatalization of velars, was originally soft; in these languages it hardened and retracted to the modern postalveolar [ʐ], which cannot be palatalized in native words, as in Russian жена [ʐɨˈna], Belarusian жонка [ˈʐonka] and Polish żona [ˈʐɔna] 'wife'. Ukrainian, Czech and the South Slavic languages keep a palato-alveolar [ʒ].",
  "phoneticEffects": "Retraction, Retroflexion, Depalatalization",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Russian",
      "languageFamily": "East Slavic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Slavic *žena [ʒ] 'woman, wife'",
          "to": "жена [ʐɨˈna] 'wife'",
          "note": "Russian /ʐ/ was formerly soft and is now always hard in native words; following и is pronounced [ɨ]."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Slavic *žukъ [ʒ] 'beetle'",
          "to": "жук [ʐuk] 'beetle'"
        }
      ]
    },
